{"name":"Shrubbery","hex":"#a9c08a","fetched":"2026-10-13","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"shrubbery","whole":true,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A planting of shrubs; a wide border to a garden where shrubs are thickly planted; or a similar larger area with a path winding through it.","Shrubs collectively."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/shrubbery"}],"entries":[{"title":"Shrubbery","blocks":[{"text":"A shrubbery, shrub border or shrub garden is a part of a garden where shrubs, mostly flowering species, are thickly planted. The original shrubberies were mostly sections of large gardens, with one or more paths winding through it, a less-remembered aspect of the English landscape garden with very few original 18th-century examples surviving. As the fashion spread to smaller gardens, linear shrub borders covered up walls and fences, and were typically underplanted with smaller herbaceous flowering plants. By the late 20th century, shrubs, trees and smaller plants tend to be mixed together in the most visible parts of the garden, hopefully blending successfully. At the same time, shrubs, especially very large ones, have become part of the woodland garden, mixed in with trees, both native species and imported ornamental varieties."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/7/78/Sheringham_Park_1.JPG/330px-Sheringham_Park_1.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Shrubbery","wikidata":"Q7504315"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/b8/A_shrubbery_on_Tregenna_Estate_-_geograph.org.uk_-_1551913.jpg/500px-A_shrubbery_on_Tregenna_Estate_-_geograph.org.uk_-_1551913.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/b8/A_shrubbery_on_Tregenna_Estate_-_geograph.org.uk_-_1551913.jpg/500px-A_shrubbery_on_Tregenna_Estate_-_geograph.org.uk_-_15519